<onlyinclude>A '''voltage regulator''' keeps the voltage supplied to a circuit close to its target value even when the source voltage or the circuit's demand changes. It is not a perfect voltage source: every regulator has limits on current, temperature and how quickly it can respond.</onlyinclude>

''Reading depth: [[Voltage regulator/Simple|Simple]] · [[Voltage regulator/Technical|Technical]].''

== Two common approaches ==
A '''linear regulator''' reduces voltage by controlling a transistor, much like an adjustable valve. It is often straightforward and quiet, but the voltage difference becomes heat. A '''switching regulator''' rapidly switches electrical energy through an inductor or other storage component. It can waste less energy, but may produce electrical noise that needs filtering.

For example, reducing 12 V to 5 V at 1 A with an idealized linear regulator dissipates (12 − 5) × 1 = '''7 W''' as heat. A suitable switching regulator may run cooler, but its actual efficiency depends on its design and load.

A '''low-dropout regulator''' is a linear regulator designed to work with a relatively small difference between input and output voltage, but it still needs enough headroom. A switching converter followed by a linear regulator can reduce some output noise, at the cost of extra power loss.

== What to check ==
Choose a regulator by its input range, output voltage, maximum current, heat dissipation, ripple, startup and response to sudden changes. Check whether the output remains stable with the chosen capacitor and whether electrical noise matters for the connected circuit. Consult the specific component datasheet rather than assuming all devices behave alike.

